/*
🧩 LeetCode — Problem Notes & Complete Code

Question Number: 144 (Iterative Preorder Traversal)
Title (brief): Binary Tree Preorder Traversal (Iterative)

Problem Description (brief):
Return the preorder traversal of a binary tree without using recursion.
Preorder traversal visits nodes in this order: Root → Left → Right.

Example(s) (with expected output):

// Example 1:
// Input: root = [1,2,3,4,5]
// Output: [1,2,4,5,3]

// Example 2:
// Input: root = []
// Output: []

// Example 3:
// Input: root = [1]
// Output: [1]

Logic / Approach:
- Use an iterative approach with a stack.
- Push the root node into the stack.
- While the stack is not empty:
    - Pop the top node and visit it (add to result).
    - Push the right child first, then left child (so left is processed first).
- This simulates recursive preorder traversal using a stack.

Time Complexity:
- O(n) → each node is visited exactly once.

Space Complexity:
- O(n) → worst-case stack size in a skewed tree.

Edge Cases:
1. Empty tree (root = nullptr) → return [].
2. Single node tree → return [root->val].
3. Skewed tree → stack can grow up to n nodes.

Optional Interview Follow-ups / Tips:
- Iterative method avoids recursion stack overflow.
- Similar approach can be adapted for inorder or postorder traversal.
*/

#include <vector>
#include <stack>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> preorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> preorder;
        if (root==NULL) return preorder;  // Edge case: empty tree
        
        stack<TreeNode*> st;
        st.push(root);  // Start with root
        
        while (!st.empty()) {
            TreeNode* node = st.top();
            st.pop();
            
            preorder.push_back(node->val);  // Visit root
            
            // Push right first so left is processed first
            if (node->right) st.push(node->right);
            if (node->left) st.push(node->left);
        }
        
        return preorder;
    }
};
